# Domain 4 — Prompt Engineering & Structured Output (12)

### W40 · Feedback loops

A code-review agent posts findings on PRs; developers dismiss about 40% of them. The team
wants to cut false positives over time without losing real issues. What should they
build?

A) Tag each finding with a pattern/category ID, log dismissal reasons, and analyze by
   category.
B) Raise the confidence threshold for posting until the dismissal rate falls below 15%.
C) Collect developers' free-text comments weekly and paste them into the review prompt.
D) Add few-shot examples of accepted high-quality findings to the review prompt.

**Answer: A**

Structured metadata turns "40% dismissed" into "category X is 90% dismissed," which
points at a specific prompt, rule or example to change.

- **B** — Right if errors were spread uniformly. A global threshold drops real issues in
  good categories along with noise from bad ones.
- **C** — Unstructured and ever-growing; nothing tells you which pattern to fix.
- **D** — Positive examples don't teach which constructs produce false positives.

### W41 · Feedback loops

Human reviewers correct extraction errors in a review UI. The corrected value overwrites
the extracted one, and nothing else is stored. Months later, the team can't tell which
prompt or schema changes would help. What should have been captured?

A) Per-field accuracy rates, tracked weekly, with alerts when any field drops below its
   baseline.
B) A higher review sampling rate, so a larger share of extractions get corrected values.
C) Each corrected document added to a growing pool of few-shot examples in the prompt.
D) Each correction's field, original and corrected values, error type and document traits.

**Answer: D**

Diagnosis needs the error itself, its type (missing, fabricated, format, wrong source),
and what kind of document produced it.

- **A** — Right for monitoring. It says that a field is getting worse, not why.
- **B** — More corrections, still no record of what was wrong.
- **C** — An unstructured pool grows the prompt without targeting the actual failure
  modes.

### W42 · Feedback loops

Correction logs show 70% of `invoice_date` errors come from European documents where
`04/05/2026` (4 May) was read as 5 April. What is the best targeted change?

A) Route every invoice from European suppliers to human review until accuracy recovers.
B) Add a date-format instruction, a DD/MM few-shot example, and a raw-date-string field.
C) Add a general instruction to double-check all dates against the source document.
D) Add a validator that rejects any `invoice_date` later than the processing date.

**Answer: B**

The pattern names the cause; the fix targets it in the prompt (instruction plus a
matching example) and the schema (keeping the raw string allows later re-parsing and
audit).

- **A** — Right when the error can't be fixed by changing the prompt or schema; this one
  clearly can.
- **C** — Generic; the model doesn't know which convention applies.
- **D** — Catches impossible dates, but 4 May and 5 April are both plausible; the
  validator can't tell.

### W43 · Feedback loops

After a prompt change aimed at the date errors, how should the team verify it helped
without regressing other fields?

A) Deploy the new prompt and compare overall production accuracy over two weeks.
B) Have the model grade a sample of its new outputs against the extraction rubric.
C) Re-run a fixed eval set built from logged failures and compare per error category.
D) A/B test the prompt on 10% of live traffic and compare reviewer correction rates.

**Answer: C**

Logged failures become a regression set; comparing per category shows both the targeted
improvement and any regression elsewhere.

- **A** — Aggregate accuracy hides category shifts, and it takes weeks of exposure.
- **B** — Self-grading isn't independent verification.
- **D** — Good as a final production check, but slow, exposes users, and rare categories
